Z||000012||88formula||12-31-2000||11:56 PM||||I have to say one thing, There are definitely some pyramid schemes out there but there are also legal legitimate network marketing companies out there too. Pyramid schemes are illegal but network marketing is not in America. <P>Have you guys ever heard of Amway? It is one of the biggest and oldest network marketing companies out there. I have been involved with a network marketing company for some time now (not Amway). I’m not involved with Amway. I’m just using Amway as an example. The company I am involved with is traded on the New York Stock exchange and made 120-million in profits last year so it has to be legal. I have not told you guys about this because I respect you people and Cliffs no Spam policy and therefore I won’t tell you what company I am involved with or post any information on this forum about my company. I just had to let the guys here that think this stuff is a scam know real network marketing is not a scam. <P>Real network marketing is not a get rich quick scheme and takes hard work, devotion, time, money, and the entrepreneurial spirit to create a residual income that only movie stars, rich people, rock stars, and inventors usually enjoy. Most people earn linear income were you trade your time for money at a regular job. Linear income is were you work for an hour and you get paid for an hour. Residual income is were you work on something one time and get paid over and over for that work you did that one time, which I call building equity in yourself.<P>I’m not defending this guy and he probably is running a scam because you don’t do network marketing by e-mail bombing, posting Spam on forums, and posting Spam in chat rooms. <P>No flames intended but when someone say’s  “O- it’s one of those pyramid scams huh” they are being closed minded.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||stimpy||01-01-2001||11:14 AM||||Well pardon my closed minded A$$, but when someone is makng money by first giving money to other people, with the only result being that still other people are told to send money to him, well that fits my definition of a pyramid scam. In no way can that be legitmized as multi level marketing, because noproduct or service is being marketed. Obviously, you did not read the original post because then you would see that no one was selling soap.<P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||88formula||01-01-2001||05:41 PM||||Did you know that Donald Trump appeared on the David Letterman Show recently. Dave asked him what he would do if he lost everything and had to start over from scratch.  Without hesitating, Trump said he would find a good network marketing program and get to work. The audience started to hoot and boo him. He looked out at the audience and dead-panned his response, "That's why I am sitting up here and you all are sitting out there!"<BR> <BR>Multi-Level Marketing has gained respectability. It is being taught in the Harvard Business School, and both Stanford Research and the Wall Street Journal have stated that between 50% and 65% of all goods and services will be sold through multi-level methods by 2005.<P>I did not read the original post by XenoGenn and as I have said before Stimpy, I am not defending this guy. I am defending the business that I am in. Those people in the audience booing Donald Trump are closed minded. Their lives will never change because they won’t take risks like Donald Trump did or they don’t want to. They will be just like everyone else working up the corporate ladder40-50 hours a week all stressed out making someone else rich trading their time for money. They have no control and no real financial and time freedom. Next year their company could have a big layoff.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Black88GT||01-01-2001||10:09 PM||||Hey 88formula, could you explain to me what Multi-Level Marketing, MLM for short, exactly is?  <P>I read the original post and knew it was SPAM from the get go, but I figured I would keep to myself.  Now I am just curious about this MLM thing...<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||5
Z||000016||stimpy||01-02-2001||12:24 AM||||My blatently cynical assessment of Multi-Level Marketing is this. An acquaintance will tell you of a fantastic way to start making incredible amounts of money. Of course they a very hush hush about how this is done, but eventually they will wear you down to coughing up the $500 or so to get started as a distributor. Then it is time to start hounding your friends and neighbors to purchase your products or services and also cough upthe $500 so they can become distributors under your supervision.<P>All and all, there is nothing sinister about this, and as a matter of fact, I know some people who do quite well at this.  As a distributor, one picks up a percentage of the profit that a retail middleman would make, and the rest is distributed up the food chain. Some examples of MLM are Avon, Amway, Mary Kay and The Pampered Chef. If someone has good connections in the community and some good sales technique, they can do well with this.<P>I make the differentation between MLM and Pyrimid scams in that, although the structure is similar to the MLM, pyrimids provide no goods or services. It is nothing but a scam to defraud gullible, greedy people. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||fiero56||01-02-2001||12:47 AM||||I had some dip$hit call me at work a few weeks ago, trying to get me to join himself and some doctors and such who are "making lots of money" doing something with a company with "star" in the name. I don't remmeber the company name, but remember he was very persistant on getting me to meet him so he could give me a presentation on the company, and basically recruit me to be a seller or whatever of their computer stuff. Took quite a while for him to get the message I wasn't going to have anything to do whith the setup. Reminded me of when a tele-marketer calls, and won't take "no" for an answer. That alone raises a red-flag at me, telling me "somethin' ain't right."||||reg||1
Z||000018||LarryB||01-02-2001||12:53 AM||||You don't have to let them be persistent. Just tell them you're transferring your call to Mr. Click, then hang up the phone. <P>Works for me. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||XenoGenn||01-02-2001||08:04 AM||||<BR> Sorry for all the arguments I caused, and all that. I did not intend for it to be spam (which is why I didn't put any details like the product that was selling or details of the program) I didn't want it to be considered spam, so I just left my e-mail and an anecdote of my personal experience with it. <P> I didn't know I wasn't allowed to post personal experiences not related to Fieros. I just thought some people might like to know that there are work at home things out there available through the internet.<P> But enough of that. You can continue discussing something that I gave no real details about if you'd like, it doesn't much matter anymore. I see there is no place for that stuff here at all. Sorry for all the commotion. <P> Cliff: Sorry to have brought what you consider spam to this forum.<P>||||reg||1
